@startuml
title logGO - WriteLog Sequence (Async Write Path)

participant Caller
participant Manager
queue Channel as "queue chan LogEntry"
participant WriterLoop as "run() goroutine"
participant LogWriter
participant Handlers as "Registered LogHandlers"

Caller -> Manager: WriteLog(level, message, fields...)
activate Manager
Manager -> Manager: RLock stateMu; build LogEntry (plain value, not pooled)
Manager -> Channel: enqueue entry\n(Block: wait for room, or DropNewest: drop + count)
Manager -> Manager: RUnlock stateMu
Manager --> Caller: return (does not wait for I/O)
deactivate Manager

== Background, decoupled from caller ==
activate WriterLoop
WriterLoop -> Channel: dequeue entry
WriterLoop -> LogWriter: Write(entry)
LogWriter --> WriterLoop: ok / error
WriterLoop -> Handlers: Handle(entry) for each registered handler
deactivate WriterLoop

note right of Manager
  The RWMutex (stateMu) is what makes Close
  race-free: WriteLog holds RLock for the
  whole check-then-send, Close takes Lock
  (which waits out every in-flight RLock)
  before closing the channel — provably safe
  to call concurrently with in-flight writes,
  not just "safe in practice." See
  manager_test.go's concurrent test, run
  under -race.

  Entries are never pooled/reused: a handler
  can retain the pointer past its Handle()
  call, so reusing the struct afterward would
  corrupt whatever it kept. WriteEntry (not
  shown here) is the same path but enqueues an
  already-built LogEntry as-is, preserving its
  original timestamp — see 01 and 05.
end note

@enduml
